We can install everything with the following command:
===========================================================
curl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | sh -s - --write-kubeconfig-mode 644
Now let's check the Kubernetes installation:
============================================
kubectl version


To perform this check, run the following command:
===============================================
k3s kubectl get nodes


We're now going to create our first resource by creating a Pod from the nginx image via imperative configuration :

kubectl run nginx --image=nginx
We have just created our first Pod, and can validate its presence with the following command:

kubectl get pod


create yml 
============
kubectl apply -f wordpress.yml
Check the list of existing pods

kubectl get pod



It is possible to create a Deployment that runs 10 replicas of an NGINX container with the following kubectl imperative command:
================================================================================================================================
kubectl create deployment nginx-deployment --image=nginx --replicas=10
Let's check that the Deployment has been created with the following command:
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
kubectl get all # Allows us to retrieve all available resources


Delete nginx-deployment :
=============================
kubectl delete deployment nginx-deployment



Write the following content to a deployment.yml file:
--------------------------------------------------
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: nginx-deployment
  labels:
    app: web
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web
  replicas: 10
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx
          ports:
            - containerPort: 80

spec.replicas: specifies the number of Pods to be executed.

strategy.type: specifies the deployment strategy to be used. In our case, we specify RollingUpdate, which means that if we update the application with a new deployment, the new versions are progressively deployed to the Pods to avoid downtime.

spec.containers: specifies the container image to be executed in each of the Pods and ports to be exposed.
===============================================================================================================
kubectl apply -f deployment.yml
