services
========
A Kubernetes service can be configured using a YAML manifest, more information on services is available via the documentation https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/ :

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: nginx
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 8080


namespaces
-----------
To display API resources and see which ones have Namespaces in a Kubernetes cluster, we can use the kubectl command:
kubectl api-resources


Most Kubernetes distributions will create three or four Namespaces by default. We can see these default Namespaces when we create a new cluster using the kubectl client.
kubectl get namespace
Output display:
NAME STATUS AGE
default Active 2d5h
kube-system Active 2d5h
kube-public Active 2d5h
kube-node-lease Active 2d5h

	We can create Namespaces using the kubectl client as follows:
kubectl create namespace datascientest


	Now let's check our Namespaces list:
kubectl get namespaces # or kubectl get ns


	We can also list resources deployed in the namespace datascientest by adding the -n or --namespace flag to specify the namespace name:
kubectl get all -n datascientest

the message No resources found in datascientest namespace. indicates that we have no resources in this namespace. So let's create a deployment called wordpress in Namespace Datascientest which will contain 3 replicas of our Pods.
kubectl create deployment wordpress --image=wordpress --replicas=3 --namespace datascientest

